## 1. Context Loading Policy

Use the smallest context set that can safely complete the task. Start with required files, then add optional references only when the task touches that area.

### Required For Every Run
- [AGENTS.md](file:///AGENTS.md) — Core agent rules and project boundaries.
- Relevant task, spec, issue, or user request that defines the work.

### Add When Relevant
- [Project Charter](file:///ai/00-project-charter.md) — Use when scope, users, goals, or non-goals are unclear.
- [Architecture Design](file:///ai/01-architecture.md) — Use when changing components, APIs, data flow, or deployment.
- [Domain Model](file:///ai/02-domain-model.md) — Use when changing business concepts, entities, states, or validation.
- [Repository Map](file:///ai/03-repo-map.md) — Use when locating code or tests.
- [Architecture Decisions](file:///ai/04-decisions.md) — Use when a choice may conflict with existing decisions.
- [Coding Standards](file:///ai/05-coding-standards.md) — Use before editing code or tests.
- [Risk Register](file:///ai/08-risk-register.md) — Use for security, privacy, data, reliability, or irreversible operations.

## 2. Context Budget

- Prefer precise file excerpts over whole files when a file is large.
- Do not load all specs, tasks, ADRs, and roadmap files by default.
- Stop reading when the implementation surface and verification path are clear.
- If context grows too large, summarize what is known and continue with focused file reads.
- Record any missing or stale context in the run summary instead of silently guessing.

